  - [38;5;153mapp.js:5-7[39m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently only mentions the endpoint in a comment; nothing executes it. I'd have the stub read
    [38;5;153mAppSettings.loginUrl[39m and include it in the existing log line, so the wiring is real and the active environment is
    visible in the console. Otherwise nothing reads the module and a typo in it stays invisible until the API call is
    someday written.

  [1mData flow and failure modes[0m

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m → [38;5;153msettings.js[39m runs at parse time and sets [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m → [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads it inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, after load.

  - [1mUnknown hostname[0m → production, per your answer. Worth naming the cost you accepted: a staging box whose host isn't
    in the map talks to the production API silently.
  - [1mLoad order[0m → if the [38;5;153msettings.js[39m tag is missing or placed after [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, [38;5;153mAppSettings[39m is undefined and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m throws
    when submitted. This is the cost of the global-plus-script-tag wiring; an ES module import would have made it a
    load-time error instead of a runtime one.
  - No network, storage, or auth behavior changes — the endpoint value still isn't sent anywhere, because [38;5;153mlogin()[39m
    remains a stub.

  [1mTesting[0m

  The repo has no test runner, no linter, and no build — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has zero dependencies and no scripts. So there's
  no established pattern to follow, which makes this the moment to decide whether to add one (cheapest before more code
  exists), or to keep the repo dependency-free and verify by loading the page.
